Overlay patch 0038: corrections to the root CMakeLists' `if(IOS)` block.

Make the game-level iOS PLATFORM overridable, stop the placeholder
DEVELOPMENT_TEAM / bundle id from winning, and flip CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME back
to "iOS" after the toolchain include (the fourth visionOS dispatch flip —
see item 4 and overlay 0022).

All four corrections live in one patch because they all rewrite the SAME
eight-line block, and a separate patch inserting into the middle of it would
break this one's reverse-apply — which bootstrap.sh's purity check and
apply-overlay.sh's idempotency both depend on. (Learned by doing exactly
that: the next `apply-overlay.sh` failed with "0038 neither applied nor
appliable", and the cause was a sibling patch's insertion sitting inside
0038's only hunk.)

Lighthouse's root CMakeLists.txt does, inside `if(IOS)`:

    set(PLATFORM "OS64")
    include(cmake/ios.toolchain.cmake)
    ...
    set(CMAKE_XCODE_ATTRIBUTE_DEVELOPMENT_TEAM "YOUR_TEAM_ID")
    set(PROJECT_ID "dev.net64.game")

Three problems for this port:

1. `set(PLATFORM "OS64")` HARDCODES the device platform and silently overrides
   a caller's `-DPLATFORM=SIMULATORARM64`. The symptom is nasty: configure
   reports "Configuring iphoneos build for platform: OS64" and the whole tree —
   libzip, SDL2, everything — compiles against the iPhoneOS SDK while the
   caller believes they are building for the simulator. This is the same class
   of bug as reference 0009 (which fixed the identical hardcode inside LUS's
   ios-toolchain-populate.cmake); it just also exists one level up here.

2. `CMAKE_XCODE_ATTRIBUTE_DEVELOPMENT_TEAM "YOUR_TEAM_ID"` is a template
   placeholder. It is a directory-scoped default, so it applies to every target
   that does not override it — including the FetchContent'd dependencies — and
   "YOUR_TEAM_ID" is not a valid team.

3. `PROJECT_ID "dev.net64.game"` is likewise a leftover from whatever template
   this was seeded from (net64 is an unrelated project). Our app target sets its
   own bundle identifier, but leaving this as the directory default means any
   target that does not is stamped with someone else's id.

Those three become "respect the caller, keep upstream's value as the
default", which is inert for an unmodified upstream build and correct for
ours.

4. THE FOURTH visionOS DISPATCH FLIP (added 2026-08-01, Phase 01).
   Overlay 0022 — ported from the Ship of Harkinian port — flips CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME back to
   "iOS" at each place the leetal toolchain re-asserts "visionOS": the
   top-level project(), libultraship's project(), and LUS's
   ios-toolchain-populate include. In SoH those three are the complete set,
   because SoH's root CMakeLists NEVER includes the toolchain itself — LUS
   pulls it in, inside the libultraship subdirectory scope.

   Lighthouse includes it right here, at ROOT scope, and lines 718-722 of
   that toolchain set CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME to visionOS for
   PLATFORM=VISIONOS / SIMULATOR_VISIONOS. At root scope that reset survives
   for the rest of the file AND is inherited by every add_subdirectory — so
   without a fourth flip the whole build silently takes its non-iOS
   branches. Two failures, neither of which points anywhere near
   CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME:

     a. `find_package(SDL2_net REQUIRED)` — the iOS branch that FETCHES
        SDL2_net (overlay 0004) was skipped, so CMake went looking for a
        system SDL2_net that cannot exist when cross-compiling.
     b. `set_xcode_property Macro invoked with incorrect arguments` at
        libultraship/src/CMakeLists.txt:184. LUS guards
        `option(BUNDLE_ID "Bundle ID for xcode signing" ...)` behind
        `if(CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "iOS")` at its line 14 — BEFORE its
        own project(), so 0022's LUS flip is too late to help. With the
        option never declared, BUNDLE_ID expands to nothing and a
        4-argument macro is invoked with 3. The error names a macro and a
        line number 170 lines from the actual cause.

   Inert on iOS and macOS: the guard is only ever true on a vision build.

Match-count asserted against the pristine vendor state.

--- a/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -119,13 +119,40 @@
 endif()
 
 if(IOS)
-    set(PLATFORM "OS64")
+    # LIGHTHOUSE_IOS (0038): respect a caller-supplied PLATFORM. Hardcoding
+    # OS64 here silently overrode -DPLATFORM=SIMULATORARM64, so a simulator
+    # build configured against the iPhoneOS SDK and every dependency compiled
+    # for device. Default is unchanged for an ordinary upstream build.
+    if(NOT DEFINED PLATFORM)
+        set(PLATFORM "OS64")
+    endif()
     include(cmake/ios.toolchain.cmake)
+    # LIGHTHOUSE_IOS (0038): the toolchain just re-asserted
+    # CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME=visionOS for PLATFORM=VISIONOS / SIMULATOR_VISIONOS,
+    # at ROOT scope — so the rest of this file and every add_subdirectory
+    # below inherit it. Flip it back a fourth time (overlay 0022 does the
+    # other three). Without this the tree takes its non-iOS branches: SDL2_net
+    # is looked for on the system instead of fetched, and LUS never declares
+    # BUNDLE_ID, which surfaces as a set_xcode_property arity error 170 lines
+    # away in libultraship/src.
+    if(CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "visionOS")
+        set(CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME "iOS")
+        set(IOS 1)
+        set(LIGHTHOUSE_VISIONOS 1)
+    endif()
     add_compile_definitions(
         PLATFORM_IOS=1
     )
-    set(CMAKE_XCODE_ATTRIBUTE_DEVELOPMENT_TEAM "YOUR_TEAM_ID")
-    set(PROJECT_ID "dev.net64.game")
+    # LIGHTHOUSE_IOS (0038): these are template placeholders ("YOUR_TEAM_ID",
+    # and net64 is an unrelated project). They are DIRECTORY-scoped defaults, so
+    # they land on every target that does not override them. Only apply them
+    # when the caller has not supplied real values.
+    if(NOT CMAKE_XCODE_ATTRIBUTE_DEVELOPMENT_TEAM)
+        set(CMAKE_XCODE_ATTRIBUTE_DEVELOPMENT_TEAM "YOUR_TEAM_ID")
+    endif()
+    if(NOT DEFINED PROJECT_ID)
+        set(PROJECT_ID "dev.net64.game")
+    endif()
 endif()
 
 ################################################################################
